// Wonder Lab world history data. Generated by tools/build_extras.py
// Years are never `found` — see check_history and .work/BRIEF-ancient.md.
// 48 facts across 8 sections.
const WORLD = [{"id":"modern-1","section":"modern","cat":"artifact","kind":"found","text":"The world's first photograph is a view from an upstairs window in France, made on a pewter plate. The exposure took hours, which is why the sun appears to be shining on both sides of the courtyard at once.","more":"It survives, in a case in Texas, and looks like almost nothing until you know what you are looking at."},{"id":"modern-2","section":"modern","cat":"travel","kind":"record","text":"The first attempt at a telegraph cable across the Atlantic worked for three weeks in 1858, sending messages a few words an hour, and then died. It took eight more years, a bigger ship and a stronger cable to get a permanent one working.","more":"The ship that finally laid it was the Great Eastern. It was the only vessel afloat big enough to carry the whole cable at once."},{"id":"modern-3","section":"modern","cat":"howworks","kind":"record","text":"In 1901 Marconi claimed to have heard three faint dots — the letter S — sent from Cornwall to Newfoundland. Physicists said it was impossible, because radio waves travel in straight lines and the earth curves. He had no recording and no witnesses outside his own team.","more":"He turned out to be right, for a reason nobody knew yet: the waves bounce off a layer high in the atmosphere. But at the time the claim rested on his word."},{"id":"modern-4","section":"modern","cat":"built","kind":"worked","text":"A steel box of a fixed size can go straight from ship to truck to train without being unpacked. That cut the cost of moving goods across an ocean to almost nothing. The first voyage was in 1956, with 58 boxes on a converted tanker.","more":"Loading a ship by hand cost several dollars a ton. By container it fell to a few cents. Almost everything about how the world buys things follows from that."},{"id":"modern-5","section":"modern","cat":"artifact","kind":"found","text":"The first computer mouse was a pine box with two wheels underneath and one button, demonstrated in 1968. It was called a mouse because of the cord coming out of the back, and the man who built it never made any money from it.","more":"The same demonstration also showed windows on a screen, a video call, and links you could click. Nobody could buy any of it for decades."},{"id":"modern-6","section":"modern","cat":"howworks","kind":"found","text":"The satellites that tell your phone where you are carry clocks. Time runs at a very slightly different rate up there, so those clocks have to be corrected. Left uncorrected, the position would drift by about 6 miles a day.","more":"That correction is measured, applied constantly and checked. It is a piece of physics that almost everybody uses and almost nobody notices."},{"id":"middle-1","section":"middle","cat":"built","kind":"found","text":"A castle arrow slit is narrow outside and opens out wide inside. An archer can swing his aim across a whole field through a gap an attacker can barely hit. The shape is cut into the stone. You can put your arm through one and feel it.","tryit":"Hold a rolled-up paper tube to your eye and swing it. Then cut a wedge out of one end and swing again. That is the difference the splay makes."},{"id":"middle-2","section":"middle","cat":"built","kind":"found","text":"Salisbury Cathedral's spire is 404 ft of stone standing on foundations 4 ft deep in wet gravel. It leans, it has been braced repeatedly, and there is a medieval iron rod inside it that somebody added when they realized. It has not fallen down.","more":"A brass plate in the floor marks how far off vertical the top is. Surveyors re-measure it and the figure has barely moved in a century."},{"id":"middle-3","section":"middle","cat":"writing","kind":"found","text":"Before printing, every book was copied out by hand. A monk in a scriptorium worked in daylight only — no candles near the parchment — and a good copyist produced a few pages a day. A Bible was years of work.","more":"Some finished their books with a complaint in the margin about cold hands or bad light. Those notes are still there."},{"id":"middle-4","section":"middle","cat":"artifact","kind":"found","text":"In 1086 the King of England ordered a survey of who owned what. It went down to individual plows, mills and pigs. The result fills two volumes and is written in abbreviated Latin. You can still read it in the National Archives.","more":"People at the time called it Domesday, the day of judgment. There was no appealing against what it said you owned."},{"id":"middle-5","section":"middle","cat":"people","kind":"record","text":"To sell your work in a medieval town you had to belong to the guild for that trade. A boy was apprenticed for around seven years. Then he worked for wages. He became a master only by producing one piece good enough to be judged. That piece was his masterpiece.","more":"That is where the word comes from. It meant a qualifying test, not a great work of art."},{"id":"middle-6","section":"middle","cat":"artifact","kind":"found","text":"The Bayeux Tapestry is 230 ft long, and it is not a tapestry. It is embroidery — wool thread stitched onto linen, in eight colors. It runs along the wall of a museum in Normandy and you walk beside it from beginning to end.","more":"There is a comet in the sky on it, stitched by someone who saw it. A running commentary in Latin goes along the top."},{"id":"builders-1","section":"builders","cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"The Great Wall of China is not visible from space with the naked eye. It is long, but it is only a few meters wide and made of the same materials as the ground beside it. Astronauts have said so repeatedly, and photographs bear them out.","more":"Highways and airport runways are much easier to spot. They are wider, and they are a different color from the ground around them."},{"id":"builders-2","section":"builders","cat":"built","kind":"found","text":"At Machu Picchu the stones are cut so exactly that you cannot slide a knife blade between them, and there is no mortar. In an earthquake the blocks jostle and settle back. Spanish colonial buildings in the same region fell down; these did not.","tryit":"Build a tower of dry blocks and one glued with tape, then shake the table. The loose one survives longer than you expect."},{"id":"builders-3","section":"builders","cat":"built","kind":"found","text":"Petra was not built and then decorated. The great fronts were carved straight into a solid sandstone cliff. The carvers started at the top and worked downwards. They never needed scaffolding, because they cut away the rock beneath their own feet as they went.","more":"You can still see unfinished tombs where the work stopped partway down, which is how the method is known."},{"id":"builders-4","section":"builders","cat":"built","kind":"found","text":"The dome of Hagia Sophia has forty windows around its base, so from below the roof looks as though it is resting on a ring of light. It fell in twice in early earthquakes and was rebuilt steeper and stronger each time.","more":"The building has been a church, then a mosque, then a museum, then a mosque again. The mosaics and the calligraphy are both still on the walls."},{"id":"builders-5","section":"builders","cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"Angkor Wat is the largest religious building in the world by area. A rectangular moat runs around it, over 3 miles the whole way round. Aircraft have since surveyed the forest with lasers. That turned up an entire city laid out around the temple, with roads and reservoirs.","more":"The survey works by firing lasers through gaps in the canopy and mapping the ground underneath. It found streets nobody had walked on for centuries."},{"id":"builders-6","section":"builders","cat":"built","kind":"found","text":"About a quarter of the Netherlands is below sea level. It stays dry because of dikes and pumps. It also stays dry because of a legal system for maintaining them, older than the country's parliament. Water boards were electing officials in the Middle Ages.","tryit":"Find the Netherlands on a relief map and look at how much of it is colored below zero."},{"id":"printing-1","section":"printing","cat":"artifact","kind":"found","text":"Gutenberg's great Bible has 42 lines to the column and about 180 copies were made. Forty-nine survive, and their pages are so evenly printed that for a long time people could not believe they were not written by hand.","more":"His real invention was not the press. It was a mold that let him cast thousands of identical metal letters quickly, in a lead alloy soft enough to cast and hard enough to print."},{"id":"printing-2","section":"printing","cat":"artifact","kind":"record","text":"The oldest printed book with a date on it is not European. It is a Buddhist scroll printed from carved wooden blocks in AD 868, found sealed in a cave in western China, and it is in the British Library.","more":"It carries a line at the end saying who had it made and for whom, which is why the date is known rather than estimated."},{"id":"printing-3","section":"printing","cat":"howworks","kind":"record","text":"Paper was invented in China. It took about a thousand years to reach Europe, traveling west along the Silk Road through Samarkand and Baghdad. Before paper, Europe wrote on animal skin. A single large book could take the hides of a whole flock.","more":"Printing needed cheap paper as much as it needed type. One without the other changes nothing."},{"id":"printing-4","section":"printing","cat":"howworks","kind":"found","text":"Ordinary writing ink runs straight off metal type. Printers had to invent a new one. It was a sticky paint, soot ground into boiled linseed oil. That was closer to what artists were using than to anything in an inkwell.","tryit":"Try writing on a spoon with a felt pen, then with a smear of oil paint. Only one stays put."},{"id":"printing-5","section":"printing","cat":"copied","kind":"found","text":"The press itself was borrowed. Wine and olive growers had been using big screw presses for centuries. The printing press is that machine with a flat plate on the bottom of the screw.","more":"Most inventions look like this up close — an existing machine pointed at a new problem."},{"id":"printing-6","section":"printing","cat":"weird","kind":"worked","text":"Once printing arrived, spelling started to settle down. When every copy of a word looked the same in thousands of books, one spelling won and the others faded. English spelling froze at an awkward moment, which is part of why it is so strange now.","more":"Words like knight and through were once said roughly as they look. The spelling was fixed in print just as the pronunciation was still moving."},{"id":"faroff-1","section":"faroff","cat":"artifact","kind":"found","text":"Timbuktu was a university city, and the libraries are still there. Families kept hundreds of thousands of handwritten manuscripts, passed down and hidden. They cover astronomy, medicine, law and mathematics. When the city was threatened, librarians smuggled them out in rice sacks and fishing boats.","more":"Most are in Arabic. Some are in African languages written in Arabic script."},{"id":"faroff-2","section":"faroff","cat":"travel","kind":"found","text":"The Silk Road was not a road. It was a shifting web of routes across deserts and mountains, and almost nobody traveled the whole length. Goods were handed on, town to town, and so were paper, gunpowder, printing and disease.","more":"Very little silk reached the far end compared with what changed hands in the middle."},{"id":"faroff-3","section":"faroff","cat":"built","kind":"found","text":"Himeji Castle in Japan is wood and white plaster on a stone base. Inside, it is a deliberate maze. The passages are narrow and twisting, some end in dead ends, and no two steps are the same height. An attacker cannot move fast or safely.","more":"It has never been destroyed in war or by fire, which for a wooden Japanese castle is very rare."},{"id":"faroff-4","section":"faroff","cat":"food","kind":"found","text":"The Aztecs farmed on the lake itself. They staked out rectangles in the shallows. Then they filled them with mud and reeds until they rose above the water. Willows planted at the corners held the whole thing together with their roots. Several crops a year came off them.","more":"Some of these plots are still farmed on the edge of Mexico City, on the same layout."},{"id":"faroff-5","section":"faroff","cat":"writing","kind":"found","text":"The Inca kept records without writing, on knotted cords called quipu. The color of the cord, the kind of knot and its position all carried meaning. Numbers can be read from surviving quipu with confidence. Whether they also recorded words is not settled.","more":"Around a thousand survive. Several people have spent careers trying to read them."},{"id":"faroff-6","section":"faroff","cat":"built","kind":"found","text":"The bronze plaques and heads made in Benin started out as wax. The wax object was packed in clay. Then the wax was melted out and metal was poured into the space it left. Some are so thin that European foundries at the time could not have matched them.","more":"Thousands were taken in 1897 and are in museums across Europe and America. Many are now being returned."},{"id":"voyages-1","section":"voyages","cat":"travel","kind":"record","text":"Ferdinand Magellan set out with five ships and about 270 men to sail round the world. One ship came back, with eighteen men. Magellan was not among them — he was killed in the Philippines. The man who finished the voyage was Juan Sebastián Elcano.","more":"Elcano's coat of arms was a globe with the words: you went round me first."},{"id":"voyages-2","section":"voyages","cat":"travel","kind":"record","text":"Chinese fleets under Zheng He sailed as far as East Africa. They brought a giraffe back to the emperor. The size of his largest ships is disputed. The figures come from later accounts, and shipwrights argue about whether wooden hulls that long could hold together.","more":"The voyages are recorded on a stone pillar he had erected before the last one, listing where he had been. That much is not in doubt."},{"id":"voyages-3","section":"voyages","cat":"travel","kind":"found","text":"Polynesian navigators crossed thousands of miles of open Pacific with no instruments. They steered by the stars. They read the direction of the swells, watched cloud shapes over islands they could not yet see, and followed the flight paths of birds. In 1976 a crew sailed a traditional canoe from Hawaii to Tahiti that way.","more":"They had to bring a navigator from Micronesia, because in Hawaii the knowledge had been lost. He did it and it worked."},{"id":"voyages-4","section":"voyages","cat":"artifact","kind":"found","text":"The warship Vasa sank about 4,000 ft into her maiden voyage in 1628, in full view of Stockholm, because she was too tall and too narrow. The cold brackish water preserved her. She was raised in 1961 and is now standing, almost complete, in a museum built around her.","more":"An inquiry was held at the time. Nobody was punished, because the man who had ordered the extra gun deck was the king."},{"id":"voyages-5","section":"voyages","cat":"people","kind":"record","text":"In 1747 a naval surgeon took twelve sailors sick with scurvy, split them into six pairs, and gave each pair a different remedy. The two given oranges and lemons recovered within days. It is one of the first properly controlled trials anybody ran.","more":"The navy took over forty years to act on it. The delay cost more men than the battles did."},{"id":"voyages-6","section":"voyages","cat":"writing","kind":"record","text":"Marco Polo did not write his account of China down himself. He dictated it to a romance writer he shared a prison cell with. That is one reason people have argued about it for centuries. He does describe paper money and coal accurately, and Europe had neither.","more":"He never mentions the Great Wall, tea, or chopsticks, which is the strongest argument of those who doubt him."},{"id":"discovery-1","section":"discovery","cat":"artifact","kind":"found","text":"In the winter of 1610 Galileo pointed a homemade telescope at Jupiter and saw four little stars beside it that moved from night to night. He drew them, night after night, and the drawings survive. They are moons, going round something that is not the earth.","more":"You can repeat this with binoculars braced against a wall. The moons are visible and they will have moved by the next night."},{"id":"discovery-2","section":"discovery","cat":"howworks","kind":"found","text":"Evangelista Torricelli filled a glass tube with mercury, turned it upside down in a dish, and the column dropped to about 30 inches and stopped. Something was holding it up. That something is the weight of the air, and he had just built the first barometer.","more":"The empty space above the mercury was the other shock. Most people at the time held that a true vacuum could not exist."},{"id":"discovery-3","section":"discovery","cat":"artifact","kind":"found","text":"John Harrison spent decades building a clock that could keep time at sea. The one that finally won is a watch about 5 inches across. It is in Greenwich. It and the three enormous clocks he built before it are still running in a display case.","more":"A clock that loses a few seconds a day is useless at sea. Every four seconds wrong is a mile out of position."},{"id":"discovery-4","section":"discovery","cat":"writing","kind":"record","text":"Carl Linnaeus decided that every living thing should have exactly two names in Latin. One name is the group, the other is the kind. That is why a wolf is Canis lupus everywhere on earth, whatever language you speak.","tryit":"Look up the two-part name of a cat, a dog and a house fly. Then look up your own."},{"id":"discovery-5","section":"discovery","cat":"people","kind":"record","text":"The Royal Society took as its motto a Latin phrase meaning roughly take nobody's word for it. Its whole method was to do the experiment in front of witnesses. Then it published what happened, including the times it failed.","more":"Publishing failures is the part later ages have found hardest to keep up."},{"id":"discovery-6","section":"discovery","cat":"writing","kind":"record","text":"Newton's great book was written in Latin and took about eighteen months. A friend paid to print it out of his own pocket. The Royal Society could not help: it had already spent its whole book budget on a history of fishes that nobody bought.","more":"The friend was Edmond Halley. He also worked out that a comet keeps coming back. He was proved right sixteen years after he died."},{"id":"medicine-1","section":"medicine","cat":"people","kind":"record","text":"Before 1846 surgery was done on people who were awake, held down, as fast as the surgeon could work. In October that year a dentist gave ether to a patient in Boston and the surgeon removed a tumour from his neck while he lay still. The surgeon said afterwards: gentlemen, this is no humbug.","more":"Word reached London within weeks, by ship. Operations were being done under ether there before Christmas."},{"id":"medicine-2","section":"medicine","cat":"artifact","kind":"found","text":"In 1895 Wilhelm Röntgen found a new kind of ray coming from his equipment and photographed his wife's hand with it. The bones and her wedding ring show clearly. She is said to have looked at it and said she had seen her own death.","tryit":"Look up that photograph. It is the first medical X-ray and it was taken in a home laboratory."},{"id":"medicine-3","section":"medicine","cat":"people","kind":"record","text":"Joseph Lister read that souring was caused by living things in the air. He reasoned that wounds might go bad the same way. So he began spraying carbolic acid over the operating table. He washed his instruments in it too. Deaths after surgery on his ward fell sharply.","more":"He published the numbers before and after. Many surgeons resented the implication that they had been the problem."},{"id":"medicine-4","section":"medicine","cat":"people","kind":"record","text":"In 1921 a surgeon and a student spent the summer in a borrowed Toronto laboratory. They pulled something out of a dog's pancreas that lowered blood sugar. That January they gave it to a fourteen-year-old boy who was dying of diabetes. He recovered.","more":"They sold the patent to the university for one dollar each. That way nobody could hold it hostage."},{"id":"medicine-5","section":"medicine","cat":"writing","kind":"record","text":"Florence Nightingale's lasting weapon was not the lamp. It was a diagram. She invented a circular chart that laid out the deaths month by month. It showed that far more soldiers were dying of disease in the hospital than of wounds. She put it in front of the people who could change that.","tryit":"Look up her rose diagram. Then try to say the same thing in a table of numbers and see which one is harder to ignore."},{"id":"medicine-6","section":"medicine","cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"Smallpox is the only human disease that has ever been wiped out. The last natural case was in Somalia in 1977, and it was declared gone in 1980. It was possible because the virus lives only in people and the rash makes it easy to spot.","more":"The method was not vaccinating everyone. It was finding each case fast and vaccinating the ring of people around it."}];
globalThis.WORLD = WORLD;
